{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788999659,"nonce":"qc8RVMxbSZCtNGC9+KGJpBpcZ0c=","title":"The widener changes the answer on a program with a narrow-type loop","message":"Same reproducer as the VecSplat refusal (see that issue for the source): a\n`while` loop accumulating into an `hl.UI8`, followed by a few more narrow-type\nstatements.\n\n    ASH_AIR_NO_WIDEN=1 ash --mode interp ui8.hl     a=4    (correct)\n    ash --mode interp ui8.hl                        a=28   (wrong)\n\n4 is what the arithmetic says -- 250 + 10 increments is 260, and 260 masked to a\nbyte is 4 -- and it is what `ash --build` produces with widening off, so two\nindependent paths agree on it. Turning the widener on changes the printed value\nand nothing else changes.\n\nThe AIR report shows widen firing exactly once on this program (`replaced=1`).\nIt does not fire on the same loop written with a plain `Int` accumulator, nor on\na straight-line version without the loop, and both of those give the right\nanswer either way. So the trigger is narrow and the guard that should refuse this\nshape is missing rather than merely conservative.\n\nWhere NOT to start: findex 24, which the AOT refusal names, is a 3-op\nconstructor with no loop. The widened code reaches it through the inliner,\nwhich runs before the widener at O3. The loop itself is in findex 22.\n\nWorth knowing that the widener already carries soundness rules added after\nearlier wrong answers; this is a shape they do not cover.","files":null}]}